{"url": "https://api.github.com/repos/gmkurtzer/ginux/pulls/7", "id": 5091272134, "node_id": "PR_kwDOVESuUM8AAAABL3alxg", "html_url": "https://github.com/gmkurtzer/ginux/pull/7", "diff_url": "https://github.com/gmkurtzer/ginux/pull/7.diff", "patch_url": "https://github.com/gmkurtzer/ginux/pull/7.patch", "issue_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/gmkurtzer/ginux/issues/7", "commits_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/gmkurtzer/ginux/pulls/7/commits", "review_comments_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/gmkurtzer/ginux/pulls/7/comments", "review_comment_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/gmkurtzer/ginux/pulls/comments{/number}", "comments_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/gmkurtzer/ginux/issues/7/comments", "statuses_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/gmkurtzer/ginux/statuses/d1f2ae6459fd0fccce255d81162225c131ee5899", "number": 7, "state": "closed", "locked": false, "title": "Harden kernel entry, state ownership, and SMP operation", "user": {"login": "gmkurtzer", "id": 13984965, "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjEzOTg0OTY1", "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13984965?v=4", "gravatar_id": "", "url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer", "html_url": "https://github.com/gmkurtzer", "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/followers", "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/following{/other_user}", "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/gists{/gist_id}", "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/starred{/owner}{/repo}", "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/subscriptions", "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/orgs", "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/repos", "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/events{/privacy}", "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/received_events", "type": "User", "user_view_type": "public", "site_admin": false}, "body": "## Summary\n\nThis PR turns external kernel-review feedback into an eleven-gate HDAE hardening experiment. It hardens privilege entry and userspace access, replaces ambient global state with explicit ownership, decomposes the x86-64 implementation, adds synchronization and SMP operation, and requalifies the complete system.\n\n## Completed gates\n\n1. **Baseline and claim reproduction** — preserved the published baseline, reproduced each review claim with deterministic evidence, and inventoried writable global state.\n2. **Privilege-entry hardening** — moved `SYSCALL` entry onto trusted per-CPU state before stack use, added guarded NMI/double-fault/machine-check ISTs, and validated return state.\n3. **Fault-recovering userspace access** — added recoverable user-copy primitives so invalid userspace pointers return faults instead of panicking the kernel.\n4. **Global-state classification** — classified every writable global by owner, lifetime, synchronization, teardown, and migration disposition, with architecture enforcement.\n5. **Runtime-state ownership** — consolidated domain, scheduler, process, socket, signal, keyring, and AIO state under explicit lifecycle owners.\n6. **Platform/service ownership** — consolidated network, storage, image, memory, paging, interrupt, timer, and service state under explicit host, domain, or per-CPU owners.\n7. **x86-64 decomposition** — split the former monolithic CPU module into architecture, ABI, execution, syscall, time, futex, polling, and signal components while preserving behavior.\n8. **Synchronization and SMP readiness** — added ranked spinlocks and IRQ-safe locks, protected the frame allocator, enforced lock ordering, and audited interrupt masking.\n9. **SMP operation** — added AP discovery/startup, per-CPU protected state and stacks, IPIs, TLB shootdowns, cross-CPU Linux execution, and 1/2/4-vCPU qualification; QEMU tests now default to two vCPUs.\n10. **Qualification and comparison** — passed the frozen static, ABI, storage, OCI, Rocky, package-network, multi-domain, SMP, stress, and benchmark matrix and preserved the results and limitations.\n11. **Matched topology benchmarks** — measured Rocky on Linux and Ginux at 1, 2, and 4 vCPUs with fixed and scaled thread counts using the same pinned sysbench binary. Two benchmark-discovered correctness failures were fixed in bounded follow-up gates: shared-writable-page TLB coherence (11.5) and exact cross-thread register restoration (11.6).\n\n## Validation\n\nThe completed qualification includes:\n\n- `make check`\n- `make architecture-check`\n- deterministic feedback reproduction\n- full QEMU boot and Linux ABI suites\n- 1-, 2-, and 4-vCPU SMP tests plus repeated 2-vCPU stress runs\n- storage, OCI cache, Rocky systemd, controller responsiveness, `dnf`, and multi-domain tests\n- matched Linux/Ginux topology benchmarks with nine samples per sysbench cell and three samples per boot cell\n\nDetailed qualification evidence is recorded in `docs/feedback-hardening/GATE-10-QUALIFICATION.md`. The topology comparison is in `docs/feedback-hardening/GATE-11-TOPOLOGY-BENCHMARKS.md`, with reduced TSV evidence under `docs/feedback-hardening/gate-11-results/` and the complete gate history under `hdae/gates-done/`.\n\n## Matched topology benchmark results\n\nEnvironment: Rocky Linux 10 userspace, QEMU 10.1.2 x86-64 TCG on Apple Silicon, Q35, 2 GiB RAM, and an identical pinned sysbench binary on both kernels. Throughput ratios are Ginux/Linux; mutex ratios use Linux/Ginux because lower time is better. A value above `1.0x` favors Ginux.\n\n### Fixed at one workload thread\n\n| vCPUs | CPU | Memory | Threads | Mutex |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 1.192x | 1.194x | 0.977x | 1.104x |\n| 2 | 1.006x | 1.110x | 0.311x | 1.055x |\n| 4 | 1.029x | 1.121x | 0.324x | 1.066x |\n\n### Workload threads scaled with vCPUs\n\n| vCPUs/threads | CPU | Memory | Threads | Mutex |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1/1 | 1.192x | 1.194x | 0.977x | 1.104x |\n| 2/2 | 1.278x | 1.033x | 0.863x | 0.781x |\n| 4/4 | 0.658x | 0.839x | 0.085x | 0.425x |\n\nCPU and memory are competitive at one and two vCPUs in this translated environment. Four-thread results expose the principal remaining limitation: Ginux reaches 0.658x Linux CPU throughput, 0.839x memory throughput, 0.085x thread-yield throughput, and 0.425x favorable mutex performance. This is a measured scheduling and yield scalability gap, not a claim of native-hardware or general kernel performance.\n\nBoot medians were 46.721/14.517 seconds at one vCPU, 34.268/16.032 seconds at two, and 31.825/15.940 seconds at four for Rocky on Linux/Ginux respectively. These are complete-path observations rather than a kernel-speed comparison because the firmware, kernel, initramfs, storage, and userspace-startup paths differ.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis closes the defined feedback-hardening research gates. It does not claim production security, complete Linux compatibility, arbitrary hardware support, native-hardware performance, or unconstrained SMP scalability.\n", "labels": [], "milestone": null, "active_lock_reason": null, "created_at": "2026-10-30T21:30:09Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-31T05:29:59Z", "closed_at": "2026-10-31T01:57:52Z", "merged_at": "2026-10-31T01:57:52Z", "merge_commit_sha": "a2a1eace615705d6b976a3ca93998738d39f57ec", "assignee": null, "assignees": [], "requested_reviewers": [], "requested_teams": [], "head": {"label": "gmkurtzer:codex/feedback-hardening", "ref": "codex/feedback-hardening", "repo": {"id": 1413787216, "node_id": "R_kgDOVESuUA", "name": "ginux", "full_name": "gmkurtzer/ginux", "owner": {"login": "gmkurtzer", "id": 13984965, "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjEzOTg0OTY1", "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13984965?v=4", "gravatar_id": "", "url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer", "html_url": "https://github.com/gmkurtzer", "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/followers", "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/gmkurtzer/following{/other_user}", "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/use